1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,810
Imagine I offer to sell you a barrel
of oil. There's just one problem.

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:06,810
I don't own the oil.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:09,030
In fact, I don't even want the oil.

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,850
I just think its price is going down.

5
00:00:12,621 --> 00:00:14,231
So I sell it to you today...

6
00:00:14,861 --> 00:00:17,671
and I plan to buy it back
later, hopefully for less.

7
00:00:18,521 --> 00:00:20,971
If that sounds backwards,
that's because it is.

8
00:00:21,752 --> 00:00:23,262
Welcome to short selling.

9
00:00:25,762 --> 00:00:28,192
Normally, making money
is pretty intuitive.

10
00:00:29,142 --> 00:00:30,742
You buy something for ten dollars.

11
00:00:30,822 --> 00:00:33,382
You sell it for twenty. Congratulations.

12
00:00:33,462 --> 00:00:35,032
You made ten dollars.

13
00:00:36,123 --> 00:00:40,413
Short selling flips the whole
thing upside down. You sell first.

14
00:00:41,063 --> 00:00:42,613
And only later do you buy.

15
00:00:43,391 --> 00:00:44,881
The idea is simple.

16
00:00:45,551 --> 00:00:48,361
Suppose oil is trading at
eighty dollars a barrel.

17
00:00:49,131 --> 00:00:50,941
I think the price is going to fall.

18
00:00:51,726 --> 00:00:54,556
So I sell an oil contract
for eighty dollars.

19
00:00:54,686 --> 00:00:56,836
Then the price falls to sixty.

20
00:00:57,646 --> 00:00:59,216
I buy it back for sixty.

21
00:00:59,997 --> 00:01:03,847
And I keep the twenty-dollar
difference. At least in theory.

22
00:01:06,207 --> 00:01:08,437
Because there's a rather important detail.

23
00:01:09,367 --> 00:01:10,857
What happens if I'm wrong?

24
00:01:11,607 --> 00:01:14,737
If oil goes from eighty to one
hundred dollars, I'm in trouble.

25
00:01:15,565 --> 00:01:17,135
I sold something for eighty...

26
00:01:17,825 --> 00:01:22,015
and now I have to buy it back for
one hundred. I lose twenty dollars.

27
00:01:23,096 --> 00:01:26,056
And if oil goes to two hundred?
I lose one hundred and twenty.

28
00:01:26,136 --> 00:01:31,086
If it goes to five hundred?
Well... you get the idea.

29
00:01:32,351 --> 00:01:36,601
When you buy something normally, the most
you can lose is what you paid for it.

30
00:01:37,391 --> 00:01:39,331
With a short position,
there is theoretically

31
00:01:39,411 --> 00:01:41,961
no upper limit to how much you can lose.

32
00:01:42,775 --> 00:01:46,125
Because there is no upper limit
to how high the price can go.

33
00:01:46,555 --> 00:01:49,445
And this creates a very strange asymmetry.

34
00:01:50,233 --> 00:01:54,303
If I buy oil for eighty dollars and
it falls to zero, I lose eighty.

35
00:01:54,613 --> 00:01:58,573
But if I short oil at eighty and
it goes to one hundred and sixty...

36
00:01:58,653 --> 00:02:00,543
I've lost eighty.

37
00:02:01,219 --> 00:02:02,779
If it goes to eight hundred...

38
00:02:02,859 --> 00:02:06,669
I've lost seven hundred and
twenty. The upside is limited.

39
00:02:07,439 --> 00:02:08,889
The downside isn't.

40
00:02:09,539 --> 00:02:11,149
So why would anyone do this?

41
00:02:14,049 --> 00:02:16,619
Because markets aren't
just about making money.

42
00:02:17,309 --> 00:02:19,279
Sometimes you want to get rid of risk.

43
00:02:20,206 --> 00:02:21,696
Imagine you're an airline.

44
00:02:22,406 --> 00:02:24,276
Fuel is one of your biggest costs.

45
00:02:24,926 --> 00:02:27,936
And you're worried that oil
prices might suddenly rise.

46
00:02:28,839 --> 00:02:32,949
So you can buy futures contracts that
effectively lock in a price for oil.

47
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:36,529
If oil goes up, your fuel
becomes more expensive...

48
00:02:37,539 --> 00:02:39,669
but your futures position makes money.

49
00:02:40,445 --> 00:02:44,355
You've exchanged an uncertain future
price for a more predictable one.

50
00:02:45,045 --> 00:02:47,115
Someone else takes the other side.

51
00:02:47,825 --> 00:02:50,675
Maybe they're a speculator
who thinks oil will fall.

52
00:02:51,467 --> 00:02:54,437
Maybe they're another company
with the opposite risk.

53
00:02:55,107 --> 00:02:56,737
Maybe they're a market maker.

54
00:02:57,645 --> 00:03:00,435
They don't have to believe
the exact same thing you do.

55
00:03:00,785 --> 00:03:04,215
They just have to be willing to
take the other side of the contract.

56
00:03:05,185 --> 00:03:07,455
And this is where things
start getting weird.

57
00:03:10,035 --> 00:03:11,985
Because when we say we're trading oil...

58
00:03:12,275 --> 00:03:13,695
we often aren't trading oil.

59
00:03:13,775 --> 00:03:15,925
We're trading a contract.

60
00:03:16,905 --> 00:03:19,495
A futures contract can say, essentially.

61
00:03:20,545 --> 00:03:22,965
At a certain date, under
certain conditions, one

62
00:03:23,045 --> 00:03:25,535
party will deliver a
certain amount of oil...

63
00:03:26,185 --> 00:03:28,135
and another party will accept it.

64
00:03:28,809 --> 00:03:30,659
But here's the important part.

65
00:03:31,669 --> 00:03:35,899
Most futures contracts never result in
somebody receiving a truck full of oil.

66
00:03:36,629 --> 00:03:40,159
The traders simply exit their
positions before the contract expires.

67
00:03:40,789 --> 00:03:43,719
So I can sell a futures
contract for oil...

68
00:03:43,989 --> 00:03:46,539
and later buy the same contract back.

69
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:49,490
No barrel needs to travel anywhere.

70
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:52,100
The gain or loss is settled financially.

71
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:56,050
And suddenly our original
question gets even stranger.

72
00:03:58,850 --> 00:04:02,600
Does the oil actually have
to exist? Not necessarily.

73
00:04:04,156 --> 00:04:08,406
A futures contract can refer to oil
that will be produced in the future.

74
00:04:09,276 --> 00:04:12,666
The oil doesn't have to be sitting
in a warehouse with your name on it.

75
00:04:13,363 --> 00:04:16,393
And if everyone closes their
positions before delivery...

76
00:04:17,203 --> 00:04:20,153
the physical oil may never
enter the transaction at all.

77
00:04:20,849 --> 00:04:22,779
What we're really trading is a promise.

78
00:04:23,649 --> 00:04:25,829
A standardised promise that has a price.

79
00:04:25,909 --> 00:04:27,409
And once you realise that...

80
00:04:27,489 --> 00:04:30,239
you start seeing these
contracts everywhere.

81
00:04:32,949 --> 00:04:37,659
But surely there has to be some connection
to the real world. And there is.

82
00:04:38,466 --> 00:04:40,956
There are companies that
actually produce oil.

83
00:04:41,446 --> 00:04:44,416
There are refineries
that actually consume it.

84
00:04:45,113 --> 00:04:48,233
There are pipelines, ships,
storage tanks and millions

85
00:04:48,313 --> 00:04:51,483
of barrels of physical crude
moving around the planet.

86
00:04:52,249 --> 00:04:55,879
The financial market doesn't
float completely free of reality.

87
00:04:56,909 --> 00:04:59,389
If the futures price becomes
wildly different from the

88
00:04:59,469 --> 00:05:02,579
physical price, traders
can exploit the difference.

89
00:05:03,483 --> 00:05:06,433
They can buy the cheap thing
and sell the expensive thing.

90
00:05:07,203 --> 00:05:08,673
That's called arbitrage.

91
00:05:09,405 --> 00:05:13,195
So physical reality still
acts as an anchor. Usually.

92
00:05:16,024 --> 00:05:17,524
But what happens when the physical world

93
00:05:17,604 --> 00:05:21,534
suddenly can't keep up with
the financial one? Well...

94
00:05:21,704 --> 00:05:24,014
we actually got to see that happen.

95
00:05:24,749 --> 00:05:29,539
In April twenty twenty, during the COVID
nineteen pandemic, oil demand collapsed.

96
00:05:30,449 --> 00:05:33,879
People stopped driving.
Airlines stopped flying.

97
00:05:34,729 --> 00:05:36,199
Factories shut down.

98
00:05:36,883 --> 00:05:39,373
But oil production couldn't
simply stop overnight.

99
00:05:39,743 --> 00:05:43,873
And suddenly there was a problem.
There was too much oil...

100
00:05:44,203 --> 00:05:45,893
and not enough storage space.

101
00:05:46,741 --> 00:05:48,211
The oil wasn't imaginary.

102
00:05:48,761 --> 00:05:50,111
It was very real.

103
00:05:50,561 --> 00:05:52,411
There was just nowhere to put it.

104
00:05:52,601 --> 00:05:55,231
And something absolutely bizarre happened.

105
00:05:56,038 --> 00:06:02,488
The price of a major oil futures contract
briefly fell below zero. Yes. Below zero.

106
00:06:05,628 --> 00:06:10,818
Traders were effectively willing to pay
someone else to take the contract. Why?

107
00:06:11,657 --> 00:06:13,017
Because if you were still holding the

108
00:06:13,097 --> 00:06:15,347
contract when it reached
physical delivery...

109
00:06:16,211 --> 00:06:19,361
you could end up with an
obligation to take delivery of oil.

110
00:06:20,131 --> 00:06:21,811
And if you had nowhere to put it...

111
00:06:21,891 --> 00:06:24,321
that was a very expensive problem.

112
00:06:25,150 --> 00:06:27,160
For a moment, the question wasn't.

113
00:06:27,850 --> 00:06:30,960
How much will you pay
me for my oil? It was.

114
00:06:31,810 --> 00:06:34,900
How much will I pay you to
take this problem away from me?

115
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:41,970
And this is where finance starts feeling
less like buying and selling things...

116
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:43,930
and more like trading obligations.

117
00:06:45,253 --> 00:06:46,803
But we can go even further.

118
00:06:47,013 --> 00:06:49,663
Because if you can trade
a contract on oil...

119
00:06:49,873 --> 00:06:52,083
you don't necessarily need oil.

120
00:06:52,890 --> 00:06:58,400
So what else can we put into a contract?
Interest rates. Currencies. Stock indexes.

121
00:06:58,750 --> 00:07:01,500
Inflation. Credit risk. Even weather.

122
00:07:02,227 --> 00:07:05,337
And eventually... something
even more abstract.

123
00:07:06,107 --> 00:07:08,057
The probability of an event happening.

124
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:11,977
Imagine a market asking.

125
00:07:12,887 --> 00:07:14,817
Will candidate X win the election?

126
00:07:15,587 --> 00:07:18,497
A contract might pay one
dollar if the answer is yes...

127
00:07:19,227 --> 00:07:21,457
and zero dollars if the answer is no.

128
00:07:22,203 --> 00:07:26,313
If that contract trades for seventy
cents, the market is roughly saying.

129
00:07:26,883 --> 00:07:29,793
We think there's about a
seventy percent chance.

130
00:07:30,578 --> 00:07:33,748
You can buy it if you think
the real probability is higher.

131
00:07:34,538 --> 00:07:36,508
You can sell it if you think it's lower.

132
00:07:37,289 --> 00:07:40,739
Now you're not trading oil.
You're not trading gold.

133
00:07:41,269 --> 00:07:43,039
You're not even trading a company.

134
00:07:43,809 --> 00:07:45,899
You're trading a belief about the future.

135
00:07:46,671 --> 00:07:48,871
Platforms such as
Polymarket have made this

136
00:07:48,951 --> 00:07:51,401
kind of prediction market highly visible.

137
00:07:52,011 --> 00:07:55,001
And suddenly the progression
looks almost ridiculous.

138
00:07:56,071 --> 00:07:57,441
First, we traded things.

139
00:07:58,351 --> 00:08:00,261
Then we traded contracts for things.

140
00:08:00,871 --> 00:08:05,641
Then we traded contracts for things that
didn't exist yet. Then we traded risk.

141
00:08:06,471 --> 00:08:08,801
And now... we can trade probabilities.

142
00:08:12,531 --> 00:08:14,921
So let's go back to our original question.

143
00:08:15,791 --> 00:08:19,271
How can you sell something
you don't have? The answer is.

144
00:08:19,351 --> 00:08:22,261
You don't necessarily have
to be selling the thing.

145
00:08:23,001 --> 00:08:24,391
You're selling a contract.

146
00:08:24,941 --> 00:08:29,811
A contract that represents an obligation,
a future transaction, a risk...

147
00:08:29,901 --> 00:08:32,691
or sometimes just a bet
on what will happen.

148
00:08:33,390 --> 00:08:36,840
And that is one of the strangest
things about modern finance.

149
00:08:37,890 --> 00:08:41,340
A financial market doesn't
necessarily need a physical object.

150
00:08:42,235 --> 00:08:44,945
It needs rules. It needs counterparties.

151
00:08:45,595 --> 00:08:48,805
It needs a way to settle the
contract. And most importantly...

152
00:08:49,315 --> 00:08:52,825
it needs people who believe the
contract is worth something.

153
00:08:55,525 --> 00:08:58,215
Which leads to a rather
uncomfortable question.

154
00:08:59,165 --> 00:09:00,845
When you strip away
the barrels of oil, the

155
00:09:00,925 --> 00:09:03,795
warehouses, the stock
certificates and the banknotes...

156
00:09:03,945 --> 00:09:05,975
what exactly are we trading?

157
00:09:09,315 --> 00:09:14,475
Maybe the answer is.
Expectations. Risk. Promises.

158
00:09:14,555 --> 00:09:16,465
And beliefs about the future.

159
00:09:17,295 --> 00:09:20,365
We built an enormous global
machine for trading those things.

160
00:09:21,195 --> 00:09:22,315
And somehow... it works.

161
00:09:22,395 --> 00:09:23,795
Most of the time.

162
00:09:23,875 --> 00:09:27,225
Until everyone wants the
same thing at the same time.

163
00:09:27,927 --> 00:09:31,477
And then you might suddenly discover that
the barrel of oil you were trading...

164
00:09:31,907 --> 00:09:34,297
was never really the interesting part.
